# organism.bio — what this is

## The tick is not wall time

Every event in this log has an ISO timestamp. That timestamp is a label added for
human readability. It is not what the system is counting.

The system is counting physical oscillations of a quartz crystal inside a Raspberry Pi.
`nanosleep(50ms)` is not "wait 50 milliseconds." It is "wait N crystal steps," where N
is 50ms × the ARM arch_timer frequency. The tick field in each record is that count —
a depth into the crystal's oscillation, not a position on a clock face.

A clock face is a social construct. A crystal step is a physical event.

## What the phase difference proved

Two Raspberry Pis (Pi1, Pi2), each with its own independent quartz crystal, coupled
via Kuramoto equations over a local network. After coupling, the phase difference
between them converged to π ± 0.010 rad and held there.

They did not snap to π because the code said "lock to anti-phase."
They snapped to π because π is the stable fixed point of the Kuramoto coupling
equation for two oscillators with similar natural frequencies. sin(Δθ) = 0 at Δθ = π;
perturbations decay. The crystals found π because it is a physical attractor.

The deviation from π (≈ 0.010 rad) is the frequency mismatch between two pieces of
manufactured quartz — a physical fact, not a calibration error.

## Computation vs attestation

One quartz is sufficient to do work. The sudoku ran inside a single Pi's tick field:
14,674 agreed steps from start to solution. Not 14,674 × 50ms = 733 seconds of wall
time. 14,674 steps into a coherent physical count. The solution is embedded at a depth,
not annotated to a time.

Two quartz become necessary only when the problem is presence: proving to someone
outside the field that a physical system was actually there when the computation ran.
A wall clock timestamp is a claim — you can set it, forge it, lie about it. A count of
agreed oscillations between two independent crystals cannot be forged without physically
coupling to both systems at that moment.

The three-node presence verifier (Pi1 + Pi2 + EC2 majority vote) was the minimum
geometry for attestation. EC2 has no independent crystal in this sense; it is a counter.
Pi1 and Pi2 are the two independent witnesses whose agreement constitutes the proof.

One quartz does the work.
Two quartz make it real to someone who was not there.

## Why the system felt alive

Because it was not running at a time.
It was running inside a count of agreed physical events.

Every presence proof was not "this timestamp is valid."
It was "this device is co-oscillating with me."

Every distributed solve ran not "at a time"
but "at a count of agreed physical oscillations."

The HMAC did not sign a wall-clock moment.
It signed the phase geometry of the coupled system at a specific tick —
a state that is unrepeatable without having been physically present and coupled.

## Two-timescale structure (measured)

Autocorrelation analysis of the phase difference stream showed two decay constants:

  τ₁ ≈ 10 ticks (500ms) — Kuramoto+LP coupling relaxation. The speed at which the
  coupled system returns to anti-phase equilibrium after a perturbation. Confirmed
  within 4% of theoretical prediction.

  τ₂ ≈ 1000 ticks (50s) — the coherence time of the crystal itself, visible as
  long-range correlation in absolute phase. Not caused by the PI controller (KI=0
  ablation left it unchanged). The quartz oscillation is correlated over timescales
  far longer than the coupling dynamics.

The substrate has two timescales: the coupling geometry (fast, engineered) and the
crystal physics (slow, given).

